Preliminary observation

This photograph and the contextual information provided together reveal the second bilateral pair in the monastery's compound-level spatial programme — the Reception Hall (客堂) and the Ancestral Teachers Hall (祖師堂), arranged in the same architectural relationship as the Bell and Drum Towers, and positioned immediately beyond them along the courtyard approach to the Mahavira Hall. The photograph shows the Reception Hall exterior and its interior through open doors: a small Avalokiteśvara figure on an ornate altar, with an inner horizontal plaque reading 華藏莊嚴 (Huázàng Zhuāngyán) — the Splendor of the Flower Treasury World.

Two things demand immediate analytical attention before the ICON descriptions proceed.

The first is the 華藏莊嚴 inscription. This is a direct reference to the Avataṃsaka Sūtra's (Huāyán Jīng 華嚴經) vision of the cosmos as the Flower Treasury World (Huāzàng Shìjiè) — an infinite interpenetrating reality in which every point contains every other point, encoded in the image of Indra's Net (Yīntuóluó wǎng 因陀羅網). A reception hall for arriving visitors is inscribed with the name of the total cosmos. The theological implication is precise: to enter this hall is to enter the Flower Treasury World. The modest spatial threshold of the Reception Hall is framed as a microcosm of infinite reality.

The second is the functional complementarity of the bilateral pair. The Reception Hall receives the living — arriving visitors, devotees, guests of the monastery. The Ancestral Teachers Hall venerates the dead — the memorial tablets of past abbots and senior monastics whose institutional authority shaped the community across generations. The pairing of the living and the dead in bilaterally symmetric halls of identical architecture, positioned on the approach axis to the monastery's principal hall, is a spatial and theological statement of unusual depth, addressed in the relational and interpretive analysis below.

Grammar note — memorial tablets as a special case

The Ancestral Teachers Hall introduces a category of sacred object that the grammar has not yet been asked to handle directly: memorial tablets (pái wèi 牌位). These are not deity icons in the strict RFIG sense — they do not represent cosmological or soteriological figures within the Buddhist pantheon — but they are not mere ritual furniture either. They represent named individuals whose spiritual authority within the community is institutionally recognized and whose presence is actively maintained through ritual veneration. They occupy the ICON productions through their commemorative function but require a note that their scale, position, and orientation are determined by institutional protocol rather than iconographic programme.

